Feature: Data Standardization for delivery_dt in f_order Table

  Background:
    Given the f_order table is located at /Workspace/stg/scripts/f_order.sql
    And the table resides in Unity Catalog schema purgo_playground

  Scenario: Validate delivery_dt format and datatype
    Given the f_order table with delivery_dt column
    When the SQL logic is applied to convert delivery_dt values
    Then all delivery_dt values should be in Decimal(38,0) format
    And all values should follow the yyyymmdd date structure

  Scenario: Handling non-compliant delivery_dt records
    Given the f_order table with potential non-compliant delivery_dt values
    When the SQL query identifies records not matching Decimal(38,0) or yyyymmdd
    Then an error message "Invalid delivery_dt format" will be logged
    And the non-compliant records will be flagged for review

  Scenario Outline: Validate delivery_dt with various data inputs
    Given a record in f_order table with delivery_dt value '<date_value>'
    When the SQL check is executed
    Then the result should be '<expected_result>'
  
    Examples:
      | date_value | expected_result       |
      | 20240910   | Valid                 |
      | 20249300   | Invalid delivery_dt format |
      | ABCD1234   | Invalid delivery_dt format |

  Scenario: SQL Logic fails due to upstream dependency
    Given there is a dependency on the supply_chain_delivery table
    When the upstream table fails to provide necessary data
    Then the error "Upstream dependency failure" is triggered
    And the process will pause until resolved

  Scenario: Integration with engineering workflow
    Given the data standardization process is part of a larger workflow
    When the SQL script executes successfully
    Then the standardized data is passed to the next data engineering stage
    And successful execution is logged with timestamp

  Scenario: Logging and auditing of data standardization
    Given the SQL logic is executed on f_order table
    When a discrepancy is found during execution
    Then log the current state and inputs
    And provide output for auditing as "Log ID: <unique_identifier>"
